AURORA: A Prescriptive Theoretical Framework for Digital-Physical Production Systems

## Towards a Unified Model of Simulation-Validated, Platform-Mediated Manufacturing

**Author:** Ouadi Maakoul
**Thesis submitted for the degree of Doctor of Philosophy**
**Field:** Industrial Engineering & Digital Production Systems
**Date:** 2025

---

## ABSTRACT

The convergence of immersive digital creation environments with automated physical manufacturing represents one of the most consequential structural transitions in modern industrial economics. This thesis introduces and theorizes **Aurora**, a federated digital-physical production platform conceived as a systematic solution to what we term the **Digital Validation Gap** — the persistent, costly discontinuity between the digital representation of an artifact and its reliable physical instantiation. Drawing on Digital Twin validation theory, Socio-Technical Systems (STS) theory, two-sided platform economics, Actor-Network Theory (ANT), and simulation epistemology, this work moves beyond descriptive system specification toward a **prescriptive theoretical framework** capable of generating generalizable, testable propositions about digital-physical production systems. We formalize the core manufacturing conversion function F : D → P within the ISO 23247 Digital Twin framework, stress-test Aurora's value creation model against empirical evidence on platform failure rates, and propose a mixed-methods validation methodology integrating the Best-Worst Method with Fuzzy Comprehensive Evaluation (BWM-FCE). The thesis concludes with a refined risk matrix that incorporates regulatory, cybersecurity, socio-organizational, and platform-liability dimensions absent from prior specifications. The central theoretical contribution is the identification of seven **generalizable design principles** for digital-physical production platforms that can sustain the F : D → P conversion at industrial scale.

**Keywords:** Digital Twin, Digital Thread, Digital-Physical Production, Socio-Technical Systems, Platform Economics, Simulation Validation, Actor-Network Theory, Manufacturing Ontology, ISO 23247

