## CHAPTER 5 — REFINED RISK MATRIX WITH THEORETICAL GROUNDING

### 5.1 Empirical Baseline: Digital Transformation Failure Rates

Before analyzing Aurora-specific risks, it is essential to situate the platform within the empirical landscape of digital transformation outcomes. Empirical evidence from bibliometric analysis of digital transformation research indicates an 80% failure rate in digital transformation efforts. A Gartner survey finds only about 48% of projects fully meet or exceed their targets, and globally these failed efforts are estimated to cost organizations an astonishing $2.3 trillion per year. Bain's 2024 study found 88% of business transformations fail to achieve their original ambitions.

These figures are not cited to discourage ambition but to calibrate it: any theoretical framework for Aurora that does not account for the structural probability of partial or full platform failure is incomplete. The failure of the Dutch additive manufacturing software company Dimanex in 2026 — a platform with a comparable value proposition to Aurora's — provides a concrete case study demonstrating that technological soundness is a necessary but not sufficient condition for platform viability.

### 5.2 Comprehensive Risk Matrix

| Risk Category | Mechanism | Theoretical Impact | Mitigation Strategy |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Simulation Accuracy Gap** | FEA error rates vary by material class and geometry complexity; no universal 95% target is achievable | Reduces α in V_extended; increases manufacturing defect rates; increases platform liability | SSB framework: apply simulation-sufficiency criteria by design class; hybrid validation for Regions II–III |
| **Blockchain Scalability** | Ethereum L2 transaction throughput constraints; latency under high concurrent order volume | Increases t_delay in DRDM; reduces creator satisfaction; undermines real-time royalty premise | Federated shard architecture; off-chain computation with on-chain settlement; benchmark throughput at 10,000 concurrent transactions |
| **IP Theft Rate** | Current digital design theft rate estimated at 15%; zero-knowledge proofs reduce but do not eliminate exposure | Reduces ρ_IP discount term in V_extended; undermines creator trust; reduces platform adoption | Layered defense: ZKP + blockchain watermarking + legal framework + economic disincentive (make licensing cheaper than theft) |
| **Manufacturing Partner Adoption** | STS barriers: security concerns, competence gaps, standards resistance, high integration costs | Delays critical mass; reduces m in network utility function; increases per-transaction cost | Phase 1: target digital-native manufacturers already using automation; provide guaranteed minimum order volumes during onboarding |
| **Platform Liability** | Aurora as "second-order lead firm": power over value chain without proportionate liability for participant actions | Regulatory exposure in product liability, consumer protection, IP law; reputational risk | Explicit Terms of Service; insurance layer; jurisdictional liability clauses per manufacturing region |
| **Regulatory Risk** | Digital product passport mandates; AI-generated design liability; jurisdiction-specific manufacturing standards | Reduces (1 − ρ_reg) term; increases compliance cost; may require regional platform instances | Modular compliance engine; proactive engagement with ISO 23247 standardization bodies; legal risk reserve fund |
| **Digital Transformation Failure** | 70–88% of digital transformation initiatives fail to meet objectives; organizational and strategic factors dominate | Platform may achieve technical milestones while failing to generate expected economic value | "Graceful degradation" architecture: define minimum viable value proposition at each phase; failure-mode analysis built in from design stage |
| **Cybersecurity / CPS Safety** | Security measures (encryption, access control) introduce latency that may conflict with safety-critical real-time requirements | Trade-off between security and safety in safety-critical manufacturing applications | Explicit CPS safety-security trade-off analysis; tiered security profiles by design class and SSB region |

