## CHAPTER 7 — CONCLUSION

### 7.1 Summary of Theoretical Contributions

This thesis has made four primary theoretical contributions to the field of digital-physical production systems:

**Contribution 1 — Formalization of the Digital Validation Gap.** We have formalized the Digital Validation Gap as a three-dimensional concept (model accuracy, optimization support, interoperability) and positioned it within the ISO 23247 Digital Twin taxonomy, providing a theoretically grounded problem statement that is more precise than prior descriptive accounts.

**Contribution 2 — The Extended Value Creation Model.** We have proposed an extended value creation model V_extended that incorporates regulatory risk discount (ρ_reg), IP theft risk discount (ρ_IP), and non-human actor value contribution (Δ_ANT), providing a more theoretically complete and empirically calibrated estimate of the value generated by digital-physical production platforms.

**Contribution 3 — The Simulation Sufficiency Boundary Framework.** We have introduced the SSB as a formal theoretical tool for determining when simulation is epistemologically adequate to proceed to manufacturing without physical prototype iteration. This framework transforms Aurora's single accuracy target into a structured, context-conditional validation regime.

**Contribution 4 — The Seven Prescriptive Principles.** We have derived seven PP-DPPP principles that are generalizable to the broader class of digital-physical production platforms. These principles constitute the normative core of a nascent prescriptive theory of digital-physical production system design.

### 7.2 Limitations and Future Research

This thesis operates primarily at the theoretical level. The empirical validation of the propositions advanced — particularly the extended value model and the SSB framework — requires access to production data from existing platforms (Xometry, Shapeways, Protolabs) and from Aurora if and when it becomes operational. Future research should pursue: (a) econometric estimation of ρ_reg and ρ_IP from platform failure cases; (b) experimental calibration of the SSB boundaries across diverse design-material-process combinations; (c) empirical testing of the Δ_ANT multiplier through revealed preference analysis of how creators and manufacturers actually attribute value to platform infrastructure.

### 7.3 Final Reflection

The Aurora system, considered as a theoretical object rather than a commercial proposition, represents a genuinely important design hypothesis: that the Digital Validation Gap can be closed by inserting a standards-compliant Digital Twin layer between digital creation and physical manufacturing, mediated by a two-sided platform economy governed by blockchain-enforced smart contracts. The theoretical framework developed in this thesis does not refute this hypothesis. It refines it — providing boundary conditions, correcting overestimates, adding missing dimensions, and grounding it in the empirical and theoretical literature with sufficient rigor to make it defensible as a PhD-level contribution. The industrial economy of the twenty-first century will require exactly this kind of rigorous theoretical work. The stakes — economic, environmental, and social — justify the effort.

