#### A.2 The Aurora Production System as a Formal Tuple

**Definition A.1 (Aurora System).**  
The digital-physical production platform is formally defined by the 7‑tuple  

\[
\mathcal{A} = \langle \mathcal{C}, \mathcal{D}, \mathcal{T}, \mathcal{P}, \mathcal{F}, \mathcal{V}, \mathcal{G} \rangle,
\]

where  

* \(\mathcal{C}\) – finite set of human creators (\(|\mathcal{C}| \approx 3\times 10^8\) in 2024),  
* \(\mathcal{D}\) – set of digital designs, each expressed in the Aurora Design Format (ADF),  
* \(\mathcal{T}\) – set of validated Digital Twins,  
* \(\mathcal{P}\) – set of physical manufactured objects,  
* \(\mathcal{F}: \mathcal{T} \to \mathcal{P}\) – the manufacturing conversion function,  
* \(\mathcal{V}: \mathcal{P} \to \mathbb{R}^+\) – the value realisation function,  
* \(\mathcal{G}\) – governance layer (smart contracts, cryptographic attestations, royalty rules).

The critical structural insight is that \(\mathcal{F}\) is only defined on \(\mathcal{T}\), not directly on \(\mathcal{D}\). The platform enforces the composite mapping \(\mathcal{F} \circ \mathcal{V}_{op} : \mathcal{D} \to \mathcal{P}\), where \(\mathcal{V}_{op}\) is the validation operator defined below. This architectural choice makes the Digital Validation Gap formally visible and closeable.

---

#### A.3 The Validation Operator and Digital Twin Certification

**Definition A.2 (Validation Operator).**  
The partial mapping \(\mathcal{V}_{op} : \mathcal{D} \to \mathcal{T} \cup \{\bot\}\) is defined by  

\[
\mathcal{V}_{op}(d) = 
\begin{cases}
T_d, & \text{if } \mu(d) \land \sigma(d) \land \kappa(d) \land \iota(d), \\
\bot, & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\]

The four predicates are:

* \(\mu(d)\) – manufacturability: at least one certified manufacturing process can physically realise the design within geometric, material, and process constraints.
* \(\sigma(d)\) – structural simulation: Finite Element Analysis (FEA) returns a vector of physical performance metrics with explicit error bounds \(\epsilon_i\) such that \(\epsilon_i \le \tau_i\) for all safety‑ and function‑critical metrics.
* \(\kappa(d)\) – cost feasibility: projected total production cost does not exceed the creator’s declared budget.
* \(\iota(d)\) – integrity: the resulting twin \(T_d\) is cryptographically signed and its hash is immutably stored on-chain, establishing a non‑repudiable link between the design and the validated representation.

Only when \(\mathcal{V}_{op}(d) \neq \bot\) does the manufacturing function \(\mathcal{F}\) accept the order. This ensures every physical object produced by Aurora is backed by a fully validated digital thread compliant with the ISO 23247 framework.

